WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.069
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-nominated

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:05.371
actress you know
from such films as "Mean Girls,"

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:06.506
"Mamma Mia!," and "Mank."

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.408
She stars in
"A Mouthful of Air,"

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:10.610
which is exclusively
in theaters October 29th.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:11.911
Let's take a look.

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:16.683 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.618
-Wow.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:21.254 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:24.757
Look at that.

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:29.195
I have a birthday boy who's
very excited to see Mommy.

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.765
-Happy birthday...

00:00:31.765 --> 00:00:35.267
Hi, champy. Happy birthday.

00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:36.402
Do you like your sign?

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:40.006
It says "Happy birthday,
Teddy Bear."

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:43.876
Mama made that sign.
She just finished.

00:00:43.876 --> 00:00:46.312
You want to see what I got you?
Do you wanna see?

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:49.415
-Please welcome back to the
show our friend Amanda Seyfried.

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.551
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:57.924 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:04.297 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:07.133
I'm so happy to see you
in person again.

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.300
-This is bananas.
-I know.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.870
We Zoomed a couple times
over the last two years.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.272
-We did a lot of Zooming,
and I did not mind it.

00:01:13.272 --> 00:01:15.008
-You were really good
at Zooming

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.076
because you lived on a farm,

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:20.212
and you would actually sometimes
hear animals in the background.

00:01:20.212 --> 00:01:22.281
And I will tell you
I liked that a great deal.

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:23.349
-I liked it, too.

00:01:23.349 --> 00:01:26.152
It felt very comfortable,
you know?

00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.922
Waist-up kind of dressing.
You know, it was just nice --

00:01:29.922 --> 00:01:31.658
I mean, I had a baby --
very young baby

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:33.759
at the time, too,
so it really worked for me.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:35.962
I love the vibe
of a studio audience.

00:01:35.962 --> 00:01:37.296
Don't get me wrong.
This is awesome.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:38.931
I can't see your faces, but...

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:40.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:43.169
I do -- I'm -- But as much
stage fright as I have --

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:45.904
I really do suffer
from extreme stage fright.

00:01:45.904 --> 00:01:47.507
I just had some wine.

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:50.910
But it really feels nice
that you're here

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:53.245
and we're all together.
-It's nice to be together.

00:01:53.245 --> 00:01:55.147
-It makes it feel like there's
some progress being made.

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:57.283
-It was -- Another thing
that was really nice

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:59.218
since the last time I saw you
is we were talking about

00:01:59.218 --> 00:02:01.153
the film "Mank"
that you were so wonderful in.

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:03.455
And then you were nominated,
deservingly so,

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:04.623
for an Academy Award.

00:02:04.623 --> 00:02:06.425
-Thanks. I know.
It was a big deal.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:09.061
-I am always --
-But I will say --

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:11.463
That -- That -- Oh. God.

00:02:11.463 --> 00:02:15.568
I was -- I got the call
from my publicist, who --

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:17.203
I turned my phone off
the night before,

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.705
because I was like,
"If anyone's going to tell me

00:02:19.705 --> 00:02:21.974
I got an Academy Award
nomination or didn't,

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:23.876
I want it to be my mother."
-That's very sweet.

00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.144
-She lives with us. Yeah.

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:26.613
But I was also sleeping in

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:30.683
'cause I had a tough case
of COVID, you know?

00:02:30.683 --> 00:02:32.718
So it was really hard
not to talk about it

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:34.486
because in the press
you have to do

00:02:34.486 --> 00:02:36.556
all these interviews the next
day, and I was like...

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.191
[Gravelly voice] "Yeah, no.
I'm really happy.

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.226
Like, it's really exciting.
I, uh --

00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:42.128
No, I, like, couldn't be
happier, really."

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:44.497
I'm, like, so ill.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:46.766
And I didn't want that
to, you know, take over --

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.868
Because, you know, clickbait
and stuff like that.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:51.403
So I was dealing with one of
the worst moments of my life,

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:53.105
worrying that my mom
was gonna get sick.

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:54.573
She had been vaccinated.
Thank God.

00:02:54.573 --> 00:02:56.943
'Cause it was in March, right?
So it was before I --

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:58.744
Actually I had just gotten
vaccinated

00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:00.646
two weeks before I got
my positive result...

00:03:00.646 --> 00:03:02.681
-Congratulations.
It's great to get both.

00:03:02.681 --> 00:03:04.750
-It's great to have both,
apparently.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.919
Hybrid immunity.
But, no, it was scary

00:03:06.919 --> 00:03:09.788
because I have two small kids
and I was worried about them.

00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:12.091
And my husband was working.
And it was really stressful.

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:14.093
And then all of a sudden,
this amazing

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.396
career-changing thing happens.

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:19.666
And, boy, life is weird.
-Yeah.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:22.334
-Makes you really
embrace the positive.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:24.537
-I would imagine.
-Not the COVID positive.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:27.907
I guess the COVID positive,
as well.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.542
-You're right.
This is a terrible two years

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:31.444
to say "embrace the positive."

00:03:31.444 --> 00:03:34.914
Dr. Fauci says, "The last thing
you do is embrace the positive."

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.516
-Yeah. Well...
-How are your animals?

00:03:37.516 --> 00:03:40.119
I know we've had some, you
know -- that part of farm life

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:43.322
is you learn that life cycles
for some animals are shorter

00:03:43.322 --> 00:03:47.426
than you might come to expect
from human beings.

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:50.896
-Yeah. I was taking
a tally of our chickens,

00:03:50.896 --> 00:03:55.434
'cause we just got back from
a long L.A. jaunt, if you will.

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.103
-And you don't have, like,
a chicken sitter.

00:03:57.103 --> 00:03:58.571
You just, like,
leave them to their --

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:00.005
-I definitely have
a chicken sitter,

00:04:00.005 --> 00:04:01.274
but she's more than that.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:04.343
She's the animal whisperer.
-Gotcha.

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:06.011
So the chickens aren't like --

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:07.580
You don't, like, leave
a fridge full of stuff

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:09.047
and you're like, "All right."

00:04:09.047 --> 00:04:11.817
-If they were a little bit
smarter, I probably would.

00:04:11.817 --> 00:04:14.187
But, no, Shannon is ev--
She's amazing.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:16.622
And I just checked in with her,
And I was like,

00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:19.325
"I don't remember losing..."

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.461
I came back,
and there were three.

00:04:21.461 --> 00:04:24.229
I left I guess -- There must
have been only been five then.

00:04:24.229 --> 00:04:25.764
But I don't recall --

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:27.267
In the last four months,
I guess we lost two.

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:29.335
So there's three.
We're gonna get more.

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.670
But it's not the season for it.
-By the way, when you said,

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.172
"I went back
and counted my chickens,"

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:35.441
I thought we were talking
like 50 to 100 chickens.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.543
[ Laughter ]

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:38.811
-No, I wi-- That's my dream.
-Yeah.

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:40.380
But I'm like --
How do you find out?

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.781
And you're like, "Let's see.

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.817
There were five, and then
we had -- we had three."

00:04:44.817 --> 00:04:46.485
I'm not a farmer, but...
-It took me a while,

00:04:46.485 --> 00:04:48.220
but I counted them
with my hand,

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.089
and there are only three.

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:53.292
I went out one day,
and I was like...

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:56.162
But there are so many animals
to look after.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:57.529
-You have goats?

00:04:57.529 --> 00:04:59.232
-Yeah. Our one goat --
I just checked in today

00:04:59.232 --> 00:05:00.667
'cause we're going
aback up tonight.

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.735
-Did you count and you were
like, "One. They're all here."

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.170
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. We have six still.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.373
Thank God.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:11.043
No, one is -- There are two
who are struggling.

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:13.846
One has a --

00:05:13.846 --> 00:05:18.317
is struggling with something
called precocious udder,

00:05:18.317 --> 00:05:22.454
where she -- her udder
gets really inflamed

00:05:22.454 --> 00:05:25.757
and heavy.

00:05:25.757 --> 00:05:27.994
And witches milk
comes out of it.

00:05:27.994 --> 00:05:30.296
It's not a joke.
This is what our vet told us.

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:31.898
'Tis the season.
-I can't believe the best word

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:34.233
they could come up for that
for "precocious."

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:35.835
-I know. It's --

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:38.337
-"We have a toddler
who is precocious.

00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:42.875
He is very swollen,
and witches milk..."

00:05:42.875 --> 00:05:45.411
-And witches milk comes out
out of his orifices.

00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:47.180
That would be terrifying,
but, yeah.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.282
-Is the goat gonna be okay?

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.918
-I think she's got
a fine prognosis.

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.019
-Okay.

00:05:54.019 --> 00:05:55.788
-I'll know more tomorrow.

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.890
-Okay.
But we'd love to have you back.

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.726
-Thank you. I love --
This is my favorite show.

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:01.426
-We love having you here.
-It really is.

00:06:01.426 --> 00:06:03.662
-What about Halloween?

00:06:03.662 --> 00:06:06.031
The one-year-old probably
doesn't have much of a take.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:09.535
-Yeah, I tell him he's gonna be
an officer dinosaur.

00:06:09.535 --> 00:06:11.136
He's gonna be a cop and
a dinosaur mixed together.

00:06:11.136 --> 00:06:12.771
-Okay. Great. Perfect.

00:06:12.771 --> 00:06:14.874
I think he will look back
on that and thank you.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:17.142
The older one probably --
The older girl, right?

00:06:17.142 --> 00:06:19.945
-Yeah, she's gonna be Peter Pan

00:06:19.945 --> 00:06:23.149
and Alice in Wonderland
and a pirate.

00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:24.350
What are your kids gonna be?

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:26.518
-The 3.5-year-old's a pirate.

00:06:26.518 --> 00:06:28.320
And the 5.5-year-old

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.623
is like a ship captain
but like --

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:31.857
-Like a boss of the pirate?

00:06:31.857 --> 00:06:34.360
-No. It's weird.
It's not like a sea captain.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:35.995
It's like a cruise-ship captain.

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.863
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.863 --> 00:06:40.766
It's very, like, brass buttons
and, like, a hat.

00:06:40.766 --> 00:06:43.436
-From "Titanic."
-"Titanic," yeah.

00:06:43.436 --> 00:06:47.507
And actually my kids
are obsessed with "Titanic."

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:49.942
This is a true thing.
And -- Here's the thing.

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:51.844
My wife is always --
You know my wife, Alexi.

00:06:51.844 --> 00:06:53.712
She's like, "Don't tell him
about superheroes

00:06:53.712 --> 00:06:55.748
because it's just, like,
very violent and stuff."

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:57.783
And then I come in one day,
and she's literally reading them

00:06:57.783 --> 00:06:59.118
a book about the "Titanic."

00:06:59.118 --> 00:07:02.355
And I'm like,
"Oh, watery grave is fine?"

00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:04.357
-"Oh, my God, you're gonna
drown" kind of thing.

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:06.792
Wow. That's, uh, historical.

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:08.895
-I think he wanted to be
a "Titanic" captain

00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:10.329
because then part of his costume

00:07:10.329 --> 00:07:13.899
is just sadly drinking brandy
while...

00:07:13.899 --> 00:07:16.302
-And kids are the best at,
you know...

00:07:16.302 --> 00:07:18.003
-They're so good
at drinking brandy.

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:20.639
-Yeah. They're the best
at, you know, posing.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:22.442
-I do think it's funny that --

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:24.176
I realize now.
I hadn't put it together.

00:07:24.176 --> 00:07:25.945
That the oldest one picked first
and was like,

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.613
"I'm a sea captain,"
and the 3.5-year-old,

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.948
who just, like, exists to be
of an irritant to him is like,

00:07:29.948 --> 00:07:31.616
"I'm a pirate, so..."

00:07:31.616 --> 00:07:33.452
-"I'm you're enemy, man."

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:38.357
-:I'm your enemy. I hope
I don't ransack your candy."

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:41.627
-Wow. What's that like?
God. I have that in my future.

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:43.529
-You do have that in your future
because they probably --

00:07:43.529 --> 00:07:45.665
What does the girl think
of the little one-year-old?

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:47.466
-She's obsessed with him.
-Oh, that's good.

00:07:47.466 --> 00:07:49.769
-Well, actually, it's evolving.

00:07:49.769 --> 00:07:51.504
-Okay.

00:07:51.504 --> 00:07:53.773
There's a lot going on in your
life right now that is evolving.

00:07:53.773 --> 00:07:56.208
The goat, the relationship
between your children.

00:07:56.208 --> 00:07:58.310
-I know, and my husband
is working right now,

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:00.579
so he is, like, in and out,
and I'm just like --

00:08:00.579 --> 00:08:04.917
I think the time you need to be
home right now is right now.

00:08:04.917 --> 00:08:07.819
Because a one-year-old
who's about to walk, it just --

00:08:07.819 --> 00:08:10.423
He wants to die.
-Yeah.

00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:12.658
-He's just,
"Where are the sockets?

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:14.860
Where is the glass?"
-Yeah.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:18.630
-"Show me where the knives are.
I'm gonna have some fun."

00:08:18.630 --> 00:08:21.967
And it's --
And he's got male energy.

00:08:21.967 --> 00:08:24.603
-Yeah, that is --

00:08:24.603 --> 00:08:27.940
That's a really funny thing.
Like my three and a half --

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:29.274
-You have two of them.
-I have two of them.

00:08:29.274 --> 00:08:31.810
I brought my 3.5-year-old
to school today.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.412
And he just -- He, like,

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:36.615
just got on the floor and just
screamed, but, like, happily.

00:08:36.615 --> 00:08:39.384
Just, like, happily screamed
and then, like, ran his feet

00:08:39.384 --> 00:08:41.553
around in a circle like he was
one of the Three Stooges.

00:08:41.553 --> 00:08:43.622
And I was like,
"I don't know what tell you."

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:45.257
-You can't.
You just got to walk away.

00:08:45.257 --> 00:08:47.059
-Yeah, just walk away.

00:08:47.059 --> 00:08:48.994
-I mean, they're not gonna learn
anything right now anyway.

00:08:48.994 --> 00:08:50.496
-That's true.
-Except socializing.

00:08:50.496 --> 00:08:52.431
-Yeah. That's what I said.
I'm like, "I'm leaving,

00:08:52.431 --> 00:08:54.466
and, by the way, I don't think
you're teaching him anything,

00:08:54.466 --> 00:08:57.202
'cause he should know
not to do that."

00:08:57.202 --> 00:08:59.672
-Yeah. Give it time.
-Give it time.

00:08:59.672 --> 00:09:01.206
-I guess so.

00:09:01.206 --> 00:09:04.176
-This film is a wonderful film.

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:07.079
It's a deeply --
It's a heavy subject.

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:08.280
-It's profound.

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.049
-It's profoundly sad.
-Yeah.

00:09:10.049 --> 00:09:13.085
-You play a new mother suffering

00:09:13.085 --> 00:09:15.954
through depression,
anxiety, postpartum.

00:09:15.954 --> 00:09:17.490
I would imagine
a film like this,

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:19.792
one, you have to be passionate
about the material

00:09:19.792 --> 00:09:21.894
and, two, they have to
be hard to get made.

00:09:21.894 --> 00:09:23.262
Like, to have somebody say,

00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:24.830
"We'll give you money
to make a movie like this."

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:27.199
-People -- We have
a lot of investors who --

00:09:27.199 --> 00:09:32.171
Maven Pictures, these two women,
Celine and Trudie --

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:35.607
they found people that were
passionate about it, as well.

00:09:35.607 --> 00:09:37.176
And they found the money,

00:09:37.176 --> 00:09:40.279
and we made this tiny
little movie in 21 days.

00:09:40.279 --> 00:09:42.381
And it was kind of awesome

00:09:42.381 --> 00:09:43.949
because it was small,
and I liked that.

00:09:43.949 --> 00:09:45.451
And we were just all together.

00:09:45.451 --> 00:09:47.820
But at the same time,
it's just -- it shows --

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:51.423
It reflects really
how we talk about,

00:09:51.423 --> 00:09:53.091
you know, mental health
in general

00:09:53.091 --> 00:09:56.528
and also how we treat mothers
in the healthcare system,

00:09:56.528 --> 00:09:59.264
which is we don't
treat mothers in the --

00:09:59.264 --> 00:10:02.635
As soon as you have a baby,
you go home, and that's it.

00:10:02.635 --> 00:10:04.970
There's no fourth-trimester
check-ins.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:07.139
There's no,
"Hey, are you feeling okay?"

00:10:07.139 --> 00:10:10.476
And, you know, your partner --
If you're a single mother,

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:13.145
you know, you're made to feel
like you have to do it alone,

00:10:13.145 --> 00:10:14.714
which is crazy.

00:10:14.714 --> 00:10:17.083
We used to --
It used to be a tribe thing.

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:19.618
And, luckily for me,
I do have that.

00:10:19.618 --> 00:10:21.320
My mom lives with us.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.589
And I can pass the baby off
with her

00:10:23.589 --> 00:10:26.359
when I'm working or whatever,
and I know that they're safe.

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:29.261
But it's so traumatic
to deliver a baby

00:10:29.261 --> 00:10:32.297
even if you're not suffering
from anxiety or depression

00:10:32.297 --> 00:10:34.900
or any kind of mental illness
to begin with.

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.402
You -- It's alw--
I feel like for most --

00:10:37.402 --> 00:10:40.038
Every mother I've ever spoken to
I know

00:10:40.038 --> 00:10:42.307
is terrified of the night times,
because you put your baby

00:10:42.307 --> 00:10:44.309
to sleep and you're just hoping
that they don't die

00:10:44.309 --> 00:10:46.112
in the middle of the night.
It's grim.

00:10:46.112 --> 00:10:50.416
But it's a reality of motherhood
and of having a small child --

00:10:50.416 --> 00:10:54.019
Even you, I'm sure it's hard
because you can only do much.

00:10:54.019 --> 00:10:55.587
-Yeah.
-You can't feed your baby.

00:10:55.587 --> 00:10:57.389
You have to make sure

00:10:57.389 --> 00:11:00.459
that your wife is doing okay
and the kids are okay.

00:11:00.459 --> 00:11:02.761
And it's just --
it's really hard.

00:11:02.761 --> 00:11:04.630
And nobody --
We don't talk about it enough.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:09.168
And I hope this movie
generates that conversation.

00:11:09.168 --> 00:11:10.902
And I hope people
understand that,

00:11:10.902 --> 00:11:12.571
that there is a lot of help
out there

00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:14.106
and that we need
more of it also.

00:11:14.106 --> 00:11:16.975
We can't be forgotten as women,
especially leaving the hospital

00:11:16.975 --> 00:11:20.846
getting those $20,000 bills,
even after insurance,

00:11:20.846 --> 00:11:23.015
and no help, no, like --

00:11:23.015 --> 00:11:25.684
Lactation consultants are
not free, as far as I know.

00:11:25.684 --> 00:11:29.488
And psychological help
is not affordable.

00:11:29.488 --> 00:11:33.392
-It's really a beautiful
and important film.

00:11:33.392 --> 00:11:35.227
[ Applause ]

00:11:35.227 --> 00:11:37.562
It's so nice that you used
your platform to tell it.

00:11:37.562 --> 00:11:39.831
-Oh, thanks.
Yeah, I hope people see it.

00:11:39.831 --> 00:11:42.868
Listen. It's not easy to talk
about this kind of stuff.

00:11:42.868 --> 00:11:46.004
But you have to talk about it
in order for people to feel like

00:11:46.004 --> 00:11:48.440
there is a place to go
when you're feeling that way.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:49.774
-I'm so happy to see you.

00:11:49.774 --> 00:11:51.443
It's always such a delight
to have you in the studio.

00:11:51.443 --> 00:11:53.379
-Yeah, thanks. I'm so happy
that I'm here with you.

00:11:53.379 --> 00:11:54.914
-I know. It's really nice
to have you here.

00:11:54.914 --> 00:11:57.283
-Yeah, I feel safe.
I am definitely not --

00:11:57.283 --> 00:11:59.384
-That's what we try very hard
to do.

00:11:59.384 --> 00:12:01.353
"A Mouthful of Air"
is wonderful.

00:12:01.353 --> 00:12:03.222
It's exclusively in theaters
Friday.

